(vector (map (:db/id -1)
  (:adr/id "ADR-2608196400")
  (:adr/title "verify-app-content-address told a human about 80 violations and a machine about none")
  (:adr/status "accepted")
  (:adr/date "2026-08-19")
  (:adr/tags "detectors evidence-floor content-address exit-codes co-scientist")
  (:adr/body
  "## What was measured

Iteration 26. `scripts/verify-app-content-address.cljs` was the next orphan from
the ADR-2608196200 census. It had been recorded as *crashing* -- but it does not
crash: it needs `orgs/kotoba-lang/content-address/src` on the classpath, which its
own header documents on line 5. Run as documented it works, and finds that **80 of
516 `kotoba.app.edn` manifests identify their document by location, not by
content** -- the thing ADR-2608157000 decided against.

## Two defects, both this loop's recurring shape

**1. It reported 80 violations to a reader and 0 to anything that files results.**
The located-only rows printed as prose, and only under `--list`. The detector
registry parses `FINDING<TAB>severity<TAB>key<TAB>detail` and nothing else. So
`SCANNED\\t516` would have satisfied the evidence floor, zero findings would have
been parsed, and a tree with 80 known violations would have been filed **clean**.

**2. Its refusal exited 1, not 2.** The script sets `exitCode 2` and then throws;
nbb's error handler overrides that and exits 1. So *could not answer* arrived at
the caller wearing the code for *found violations*. Measured by running it from a
tree with no `orgs/`: it printed `UNANSWERED` and exited 1. It now exits without
throwing, and the same run exits 2.

The second defect is the more interesting one, because the author had **explicitly
designed for it** -- the header says: *Exit codes are three-valued … 2 = the
question could not be answered … A check that could not run must never look like a
pass (ADR-2608136000).* The intent was written down, cited the right ADR, and was
defeated by the runtime. **Stating the invariant is not the same as measuring it.**

## Four directions, measured

    fixture: one addressed manifest   SCANNED 1,   0 findings, exit 0
    fixture: one located manifest     SCANNED 1,   1 finding,  exit 1
    the real orgs/ tree               SCANNED 516, 80 findings, exit 1, 8.5s at load 12.9
    library not checked out           exit 2 (refusal; was 1 before this change)

Through the tick, on the real tree: `ok exit 1 8s 80 finding(s) scanned=516`.

## What was already right

Worth recording, because the failure was narrow and the rest is a model for the
other orphans: the judge is `content-address.core/addressed?` and this script keeps
**no second copy** of that decision; worktree copies are excluded so the same
manifest cannot be counted twice; and the header says plainly why it is not a fleet
gate (its input lives under `orgs/`, and fleet ships one repo's tree).

## Registered

`manifest/orgs-detectors.edn`, 6h, 10m timeout, `:writes :none`, evidence
`SCANNED\\t[1-9][0-9]*`. No root argument is passed, so the script's relative
default keeps FINDING keys repo-relative and stable across machines. The 80
findings will land as a BASELINE on the first scheduled run; NEW/RESOLVED is what
turns that standing number into a signal when it moves.

## Remaining orphans

`d1-kotobase-migration` (17s, real output, no SCANNED protocol),
`duplicate-registrations` (55s, 10 `gh` calls, exit 0 -- needs its own reading),
`kotoba-pages` (blocked: Playwright's browser is not installed). Also open: the
three verifiers with a twin under `scripts/fleet-ci/gates/`, and
`verify-artifact-path-policy`, which skips 26 of its 27 subjects.")))
